Host VU1 hook: let the program that embeds the runtime supply the VU1 (the DON port connects PCSX2's microVU)

What it fixes: the runtime always ran microprograms on its own VU1 interpreter. This adds an optional host
VU1 (ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/ps2_host_vu1.h, PS2Runtime::setHostVu1 / PS2Memory::setHostVu1). VIF1 keeps
being interpreted by the runtime; with a host VU1 bound:
  - UNPACK writes land in the host's data memory (m_vu1Data aliases the pointer from data(), so the EE window at
    0x1100C000 is the same memory);
  - MPG (ps2_vif1_interpreter.cpp) and EE stores to the code window at 0x11008000 (write8..write128 in
    ps2_memory.cpp) keep the runtime's code mirror and also call write_micro with the exact bytes and address;
  - MSCAL / MSCALF call set_top(TOP, ITOP) then execute(pc_bytes, 65536); MSCNT calls execute(0xFFFFFFFF, ...);
    while running() says the program is unfinished it is continued in 65536-cycle slices, at most 64 per command.
    Returned cycle counts are only summed in a statistic; nothing depends on them;
  - XGKICK packets are delivered by the host VU1 to a sink the runtime hands to thread_begin
    (ps2HostVu1Xgkick), which calls PS2Memory::submitGifPacket(Path1) like the interpreter's XGKICK does, so with a
    host GS (patch 0005) they are forwarded to it and without one they reach the runtime's own GS.
Callbacks run on the game thread; begin/end are called next to the host GS thread_begin/thread_end
(PS2Memory::beginHostVu1 / endHostVu1). Without a host VU1, or if data() returns null, nothing changes. Also adds
PS2Memory::setVif1Tap: a callback that receives every chunk given to processVIF1Data, for recording a VIF1 stream.
Not handled: the FBRST D/T-bit stops and the vpu_stat bits that the interpreter path sets after a start (the
library API has no equivalent); VU0 is untouched.

How it was verified: tests/runtime/host_vu1_hook_test.cpp (fake host VU1 and fake host GS; the same hand-built
VIF1 stream run with and without the host: data memory, code mirror, starts, TOP/ITOP compared; call order;
XGKICK on path 1 to a host GS and to the runtime's own GS; continuation; the memory windows; the tap; fallbacks),
built from a tree with patches 0001-0009 plus this one, together with the five existing runtime tests. Not
exercised: a real microVU, a real microprogram, a real PS2Runtime game thread.

Based on: original code (the thread/threading wording mirrors patch 0005); the Killzone reference was only read.

